{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And [HaShem's anger] burned.</b> Far be it that the meaning of this verse be 'and He sought to kill him,' for it would have been fitting to seek to kill him if he refused to go, and not when he went, as others said. And why would He seek to kill him at the time of his going? And this is not like the matter of Balaam; also the matter of Jacob is not so. And if one contends — behold, we find חרון אף (wrath) that brought about no harm: 'And the anger of YHWH burned against them, and He went' (Numbers 12:9), and no harm came to Aaron. The meaning of 'the Levite': thus it was known in Israel, 'the Levite' [as] the prophet, for there were among them others whose name was like his name. And this matter is no diminution for Moses, only a great elevation, for Aaron will be like the mouth that brings forth the speech of the invisible soul, like the angels who are not seen, for they are not body. And behold, Moses is likened to the rank of the angel, and this is 'and you will be to him for Elohim.' And behold, Elohim in all of Scripture is none other than the honored Name [YHWH], or His holy angels, for through them the deeds of HaShem are seen on earth, or the holy ones of below who execute the judgments of Elohim on earth. And every Elohim that is a foreign god — Scripture speaks accordingly according to the thought of those who worship [it], like 'El of Hananiah the prophet' (Jeremiah 28:5)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "The elliptical phrase 'ואם טען טוען אנה' is rendered hyper-literally; 'אנה' may be a particle meaning 'behold' or a textual corruption."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:31:37.490404744Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}